%S A265279 1,5,30,196,1348,9603,70210,523732,3969948,30490434,236756604,
%T A265279 1855560840,14659284936,116616004167,933339435786,7510183191716,
%U A265279 60720442232300,493034016808830,4018764759581220,32871856582933560,269733794473967160,2219765767559949150,18316205299893247188,151505610416527986504
%N A265279 Number of pairs of binary trees with n leaves which have only a single common parse word under the grammar G = {0 -> 12, 0 -> 21, 1 -> 02, 1 -> 20, 2 -> 01, 2 -> 10}.
%C A265279 We say that a word w in {0,1,2}^* is a parse word of a binary tree T if T is the derivation tree of w under the given grammar G.
%C A265279 Parse words are counted only up to permutation of the three letters.
%C A265279 The statement that all pairs of binary trees have at least one common parse word under G is equivalent to the four-color theorem.
%C A265279 From _Alper Ferudun_, Nov 13 2026: (Start)
%C A265279 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of unordered pairs of distinct triangulations of a convex polygon with n+1 vertices whose union graph has exactly one proper coloring with at most four colors, up to permutation of the colors.
%C A265279 Here the vertices of the polygon are labeled 0, 1, ..., n in cyclic order (rotations and reflections are not identified), and a triangulation is a set of n-2 diagonals no two of which cross. The union graph of two triangulations has the n+1 vertices, the n+1 sides of the polygon and every diagonal of either triangulation. A coloring is proper if adjacent vertices get different colors; throughout, colorings use at most four colors and are counted up to permutation of the colors.
%C A265279 The two descriptions agree for the following reason. Rooting a triangulation at the side joining 0 and n turns it into a binary tree with n leaves: the triangle on that side, with third vertex k, is the root; the parts of the triangulation between 0 and k and between k and n give the left and right subtrees; and the sides joining i-1 and i, for i = 1, ..., n in this order, are the leaves. Take the four colors to be the pairs 00, 01, 10, 11, added coordinatewise modulo 2, and the three letters to be 01, 10, 11. Writing on each leaf the sum of the colors at the two ends of its side turns a proper coloring of a triangulation into a parse word of its tree, and the parse words common to two trees, up to permutation of the letters, correspond one-to-one to the proper colorings of the union graph of the two triangulations. In particular, the statement that every two trees have a common parse word says that the union graph of every two triangulations has a proper coloring.
%C A265279 A stacked triangulation (or Apollonian network) is a graph obtained from K_4, the complete graph on four vertices, drawn in the plane, by repeatedly placing a new vertex inside a triangular face and joining it to the three vertices of that face. Such a graph has exactly one proper coloring with at most four colors, up to permutation, because every new vertex must get the color that its face does not use. By a theorem proved by Fowler in 1998 (see the link), every planar graph on at least four vertices with exactly one such coloring is a stacked triangulation. Hence a(n) is also the number of unordered pairs of distinct triangulations of the (n+1)-gon whose union graph is a stacked triangulation, and the number of stacked triangulations on the vertex set {0, 1, ..., n} that contain the cycle 0, 1, ..., n, 0 (drawn on a sphere, such a graph is cut by this cycle into the two triangulations).
%C A265279 The notes file in the Links section proves these statements and derives the formulas in the Formula section. (End)
%H A265279 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A265279/b265279.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..1000</a>
%H A265279 Bobbe Cooper, Eric Rowland, and Doron Zeilberger, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.1324">Toward a language theoretic proof of the four color theorem</a>, arXiv:1006.1324 [math.CO], 2011-2012.
%H A265279 Bobbe Cooper, Eric Rowland, and Doron Zeilberger, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aam.2011.11.002">Toward a language theoretic proof of the four color theorem</a>, Adv. Appl. Math. 48 (2012), 414-431.
%H A265279 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A265279/a265279_1.txt">Notes and proofs</a>.
%H A265279 Thomas G. Fowler, <a href="https://thomas.math.gatech.edu/FC/fowlerphd.pdf">Unique Coloring of Planar Graphs</a>, Ph.D. thesis, Georgia Inst. Tech., 1998.
%H A265279 Eric Rowland, <a href="https://ericrowland.github.io/packages/ParseWords.m">Mathematica program ParseWords.m</a>; <a href="/A265279/a265279.m.txt">Local copy</a>.
